// GENERATED (pilot) — artist/album portraits: gist + portrait/liner. Crossover tier is
// deliberately absent (paused). Emitted by tooling from reviewed drafts; QC chain applied.
// ALBUM entries are being SUBSTITUTED (from 2026-07-25) by the album "what\u0027s it about" reads:
// `liner` = the Opus subagent read synthesized from the album\u0027s track reads (original method),
// `arc` = optional Fable-QC reframe rendered as an italic coda (only where the tracklist order
// changes the reading). Artist `portrait` entries remain from the original pilot.
window.ROTATION_PORTRAITS = {"massive-attack":{"gist":"The Bristol collective who more or less invented trip-hop: slow, dub-heavy, paranoid soul music built from samples, guest voices and dread. Their arc runs from the sunlit swagger of Blue Lines to the narcotic chill of everything after. You found them on 22 February 2014 and never really left, stacking over a thousand plays across the years.","by":"Opus · Fable","portrait":"The Bristol sound-system crew who never quite stopped passing the mic: from Blue Lines onward the record is built as a rotating cast of borrowed voices, longing routed through whoever holds the frame. What that longing curdles into is the through-line. Early on it's shelter and mutual care — Protection makes tenderness its argument, love as the shield raised against a world of watchful eyes. But intimacy keeps darkening toward possession and surveillance, until Mezzanine turns devotion itself into dread and retreats into a private, watched calm. The late return of Heligoland pulls the outside voices back in around entropy observed from a warm bed and a cosmic distance, love recurring less as rescue than as the one thing braced against dissolution.","note":"Mid-catalogue they put the mic down entirely — the Danny the Dog score, a whole film carried wordless. And live, the politics stops hiding: the shows scroll real data over the songs — drug prices, arms budgets, refugee names. The records whisper; the concerts name names."},"bring-me-the-horizon":{"gist":"Sheffield's great shape-shifters: they began in the mid-2000s as a brutal deathcore band and spent two decades morphing into arena-sized alt-rock and beyond, never once staying still. Loud, catastrophically emotional, endlessly reinventing. They are among your most-played artists of all, more than three thousand plays deep since you found them in November 2013.","by":"Opus","portrait":"Bring Me the Horizon formed in Sheffield in the mid-2000s and have since staged one of the most complete transformations in modern heavy music. The drift across their catalogue is one long line of softening and widening: the earliest records are pure aggression — full-throttle, joyless, with barely a trace of anything acoustic anywhere — and the records since have, step by uneven step, bled off that fury and let in melody, electronics, hooks and eventually near-total accessibility, ending in stripped acoustic experiments that sound like a different band entirely. What holds across every era is the subject matter, because Oli Sykes writes almost exclusively from inside crisis: depression rendered as an unwinnable duel with your own mind, addiction and self-destruction, authoritarian control and manufactured compliance, the collapse of genuine connection in a hyper-networked world, plague and apocalypse treated as both spectacle and boredom. The move that made them enormous was learning to make that despair anthemic — to build stadium choruses out of self-loathing without diluting either. Their career shape is the rare one that kept gaining audience while abandoning the sound that first won it, surviving the usual metalcore ceiling by refusing to have one, collaborating outward toward pop, electronic and metal alike. They are a band whose whole identity is the refusal to be a single band, and whose constant is the honesty of the wound underneath every genre they try on."},"massive-attack~mezzanine":{"gist":"Massive Attack's 1998 masterpiece and the moment trip-hop turned properly dark: dub bass, rock guitar and paranoia moving at a slow, nocturnal crawl, guest voices drifting through the murk. It is the record that owns your Massive Attack listening outright — 350 plays deep, far ahead of everything else — and the first of theirs you ever heard.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Massive Attack's darkest record turns intimacy into a form of dread. Across Angel, Mezzanine and Dissolved Girl, love keeps arriving as possession or menace — devotion that curdles into obsession, desire tangled with something predatory, a surrender the singer knows offers no salvation yet can't leave. Around that runs a second thread of watched, guarded interiority: Group Four's surveillance-shift calm, (Exchange)'s counsel that a visible face reveals nothing. What holds it together is that trip-hop hush itself, making paranoia, self-loss and wanting-what-tastes-better-on-the-way-down sound seductive rather than safe.","arc":"Played through, the guarded second thread turns out to be the destination: the record's final move is a retreat from other people entirely, into a watched, private calm."},"bring-me-the-horizon~post-human-survival-horror":{"gist":"Bring Me the Horizon's ferocious 2020 lockdown record — the first chapter of their POST HUMAN saga, made in a plague year and about one, all apocalypse, control and mental collapse turned up to full volume. It became your most obsessive Bring Me the Horizon listen: 822 plays, and one week where it was nearly half of everything you played.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A pandemic-era concept record that reads its own moment as apocalypse, splicing outbreak horror into a broader indictment of the systems failing everyone through it. The plague half runs from the cannibal diary of Dear Diary, into the contagion panic of Parasite Eve; the political half — Obey, Kingslayer, Itch for the Cure — reframes the collapse as manufactured control and calls for rebellion against a fear-built order. Underneath both, on 1x1 and Ludens, the outbreak turns inward, the real infection being isolation, self-hatred and a hyperconnected world where no one can touch. The throughline is survival horror as diagnosis: outer catastrophe as a mirror for the mind unravelling inside it.","arc":"The order keeps its coldest turn for the close — after the war songs, a deathbed duet where the corrosion proves mutual and grief outlasts the rebellion."},"pro8l3m":{"gist":"A Warsaw rap duo — rapper Oskar and producer Steez83 — who turned Polish street storytelling into cold, cinematic noir: gambling spirals, motorway smuggling, luxury that never fills the hollow. Core listening here, an eleventh-ranked artist discovered in 2016, and the pull deepened rather than faded: seen live once, in May 2026, a decade after the first play.","by":"Opus","portrait":"PRO8L3M are a Polish hip-hop duo out of Warsaw — the rapper Oskar and the producer Steez83 — who over roughly a decade built one of the most distinctive bodies of work in the country's rap: dense, filmic, and noir to the bone. The music itself, across the drift here, holds a stubbornly low mood: valence sits around the mid-thirties on record after record while energy runs high, peaking on the 2018 Ground Zero Mixtape, the release the owner returned to most. Even as the sound cools — the arc from the self-titled debut to the latest material loses energy, tempo and brightness alike — it never lightens. What they sing about, judged from the reads, is a single grim world seen from many angles: a gambler pawning his mother's pension chasing the next hand, the grey economy running along the A2 motorway, an estate saga where the only careers are rapping or dealing, luxury that curdles into a Sopranos-framed emptiness, a suicide pact spoken in first-person plural over a real crisis-hotline recording. Beneath the swagger runs a persistent self-lacerating honesty — the man who runs a crash test on every relationship to see what breaks, the witness who stays inside the squalor deliberately, the braggart demolished by a sceptical second voice. Their instinct is to deflate rap's self-mythology even while mastering it. This is Polish rap at its most cinematic and most unbearable at once, hard-boiled and image-drunk, and the owner has lived inside it for years."},"a-2yw9ix":{"gist":"Midori — the Osaka jazz-punk band fronted by Mariko Goto — married frantic piano, saxophone and hardcore tempos to a shrieking, self-lacerating vocal. Wild, funny, and raw. First heard in 2021 but the real obsession is recent: the biggest year of listening was 2026, long after the band itself had ended.","by":"Opus · Fable","portrait":"Mariko Goto's whole method is one refusal to hold love and horror apart — adoration and the wish to smash everything sung as a single syllable. The earliest 清水 material already has it fully formed, devotion curdling into last-train dread, and the major-label あらためまして、はじめまして、ミドリです。 sharpens it into feedback-hell self-portraits from someone clear-eyed enough to name her own trap and unable to leave it. Where the impulse is the entire point the songs stop developing and simply chant, want worn as freedom or oblivion worn as escape. shinsekai, the band's farewell before their 2010 split, maps that manic Osaka persona onto real geography and a nervous system that won't obey — love delivered as a diagnosis on herself, the excavation ending not in release but in a rhyme hammered flat so she doesn't sink through the floor.","note":"The missing character is the band itself — a jazz quartet playing it deadpan-straight beneath her, piano-lounge poise under hardcore shrieking. The love-horror fusion isn’t just written into the words; it’s staged nightly between the singer and the accompaniment."},"showing-teeth":{"gist":"An American act filed under his explore tier, and barely known here: a single track, Labyrinth, played through early 2026 and liked. What little the music shows is extreme — pinned-to-the-ceiling energy, low brightness — matched to a lyric of intimacy as self-annihilation. Everything else about them is still blank.","by":"Opus","portrait":"There is not much to say about Showing Teeth yet, and it would be dishonest to pretend otherwise. They sit in the explore tier — an American artist the owner has only just brushed against, with most of the usual biographical detail simply absent. What the data does show is narrow and recent: it begins in February 2026 and runs to seventy-odd plays, almost all of them on one song, Labyrinth, which he also marked as a liked track. The one reading available describes a desire so total it turns to self-annihilation, the narrator offering to be hollowed out and remade to fit inside another's hands — intimacy and destruction braided together like the maze the title names. The single track's profile is striking in its extremity: energy pinned to the top of the scale against a dark, low mood. Whether this becomes a lasting thread or stays a one-song curiosity is genuinely unknown; on the evidence here it is a recent, intense, and still almost entirely unwritten encounter."},"nine-inch-nails~not-the-actual-events":{"gist":"The 2016 EP that reopened Nine Inch Nails' late period — five tracks of dissociation, decay and violent release on Reznor's own Null Corporation. Compact and ugly by design. A well-worn corner of his library, played across a full decade from its release year to the present.","by":"Opus","liner":"A short, corrosive EP about a self coming apart at the seams, and the denial that keeps insisting none of it is really happening. Across Branches / Bones and Dear World, the narrator is already gone — memory dissolving, the world turning obsolete and unfamiliar frame by frame — pleading to be left alone to unravel. The Idea of You makes the disavowal explicit, begging to be remembered as a comfortable fiction rather than witnessed as he is, while She's Gone Away lets grief rot the self from inside. Burning Bright surrenders to that ruin as a violent, apocalyptic liberation that loses any grip on the real. The record's throughline is dissociation curdling into self-destruction, its title the running alibi."},"pro8l3m~ground-zero-mixtape":{"gist":"PRO8L3M's sprawling 2018 mixtape — twenty-three tracks of Warsaw noir, gambling, sex, and estate paranoia over cinematic beats. Their most expansive statement. It is his single most-played record by this duo, and he has kept returning to it across the years since, right up to the present.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A noir tour of Warsaw nightlife where the brag and the binge slowly curdle into self-disgust. The luxury cars, drugs, women and swagger (V8, Styl sportowy, Fair Play) keep collapsing into the same hollow reckoning — a man inventorying everything he's lost and no longer recognizing himself, kept upright only by one woman's patience (Magnolie, Byłem tam), or ticking off the conventional life that still ends in ruin (Sick Boy). Staged phone-call skits thread infidelity and domestic wreckage through it, so the excess always has a betrayal waiting back home. A party record about the emptiness underneath the party.","arc":"Played front to back, the episodes line up into one life — the swagger is where he starts, the inventories are where he ends up, and the phone-call skits burn underneath the whole way like a lit fuse."},"slipknot~slipknot":{"gist":"Slipknot's ferocious self-titled debut — the Iowa nine-piece's 1999 arrival, all masks, percussion and rage. A landmark of nu-metal's heaviest wing. He has held onto it a long time, playing it across many years, and reaches for the loaded anniversary editions stuffed with demos and remixes rather than the plain album.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Nine masked men from Iowa turning alienation into a weapon aimed both ways at once. The default mode is cornered fury — (Sic), Surfacing, Spit It Out, the demos — one-versus-the-world defiance that dares an enemy to step up. But underneath the noise runs something more disturbing and interior: the fatherless rage of Eyeless, the dissociation and self-harm of Wait and Bleed and Scissors, the derangement of Purity and the stalker's monologue of Prosthetics. The throughline is a refusal to perform sanity — dragging the sickness out into the open rather than walling it off.","arc":"Played through, the fury spends itself first: the record ends inside the dissociative trance of Scissors, the war with the world giving way to the war with the self."},"linkin-park~meteora":{"gist":"Linkin Park's 2003 juggernaut — the tighter, angrier follow-up to Hybrid Theory and the high-water mark of mainstream nu-metal. This is his single most-played album of all, first spun in 2010 and still going in 2026: the cornerstone of the whole library.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A record about who's doing the damage, and the slow horror of realizing it's you. Half its songs point outward at someone manipulative, faithless, draining — Hit the Floor, From the Inside, Numb's suffocating expectations — but they keep collapsing inward into the enemy that lives beneath the skin: Figure.09's obsession curdling into identity, Breaking the Habit and Somewhere I Belong owning the self-destruction outright. What holds it together is that recoil between blame and self-blame, and the recurring, hard-won resolve to cut the poisoned part free rather than keep drowning in it.","arc":"For all the resolve, the running order hands exhaustion the last word — the record closes on Numb, the fight ended rather than won."},"nine-inch-nails~the-downward-spiral":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 1994 masterpiece — the industrial concept album that traces one man's self-destruction from rage to the bare, broken hush of Hurt. A defining record of its decade. A deep, long-held favourite here: over a thousand plays, carried from 2010 all the way to the present.","by":"Opus","liner":"A concept album that follows a self hollowing itself out to nothing. An inner voice of ruin narrates the descent (Mr. Self Destruct), and every station is a failed exit: religion torn down as a lie (Heresy), sex mistaken for transcendence or worship (Closer), contempt for the herd lining up to watch destruction (March of the Pigs), the mechanical numbing of feeling (The Becoming). It bottoms out in the title track's clinically calm suicide and the wreckage of Hurt — degradation, disgust, and self-contamination all converging on the conviction that annihilation is the only sensible solution left."},"deadmau5~album-title-goes-here":{"gist":"deadmau5's 2012 album — long, mostly instrumental progressive house from the Canadian producer at his commercial peak, its jokey title winking at the whole enterprise. The electronic outlier in a metal-heavy library, and a durable one: over a thousand plays spread across roughly a decade.","by":"Opus","liner":"This 2012 deadmau5 record is mostly architecture, not text: long-form progressive-house builds and cold machine textures where meaning lives in how a synth line accretes over eight minutes rather than in anything sung. Words surface only occasionally, and when they do they circle one anxiety — technology that promises connection and delivers isolation. The Veldt renders a screen-built home so comfortable its children never leave; Telemiscommunications mourns a couple drifting apart through the very devices meant to join them; the Professional Griefers cuts pile war-child dread onto euphoria. Take those vocal moments as recurring flickers, not a thesis; the album's real subject is the shape and drift of the instrumentals around them."},"nine-inch-nails~with-teeth":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2005 return — Reznor newly sober, trading concept-album sprawl for taut, radio-shaped industrial rock. A long-time fixture here: nearly 1,200 plays stretching from 2010 to the present.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A record obsessed with a self coming apart. Read after read describes the same erasure — a narrator fading, shrinking, watching the line between who he was and who he's becoming dissolve (The Line Begins to Blur, Getting Smaller, Only), until even other people turn out to be things he invented to hurt himself. The addictions that promise to fill the void — a woman, a fix, an obsession figured as staring into the sun — only accelerate the disappearing (Sunspots, With Teeth). What holds it together is that dread of looking through the cracks and finding no one home.","arc":"The running order grants one late mercy — dissolution traded for a reunion somewhere beyond time, before a closing doubt that any of it was ever real."},"queens-of-the-stone-age~songs-for-the-deaf":{"gist":"Queens of the Stone Age's 2002 landmark — the desert-rock road record framed as a drive through California radio, Dave Grohl on drums. Steady company since 2012, over 800 plays across the years.","by":"Opus","liner":"A nihilist desert drive, framed as a road trip scanning between radio stations and the fake-DJ skits that heckle the listener. The throughline is a shrugging flirtation with oblivion: love treated as disposable and consequence-free (Go With the Flow, Another Love Song), living reframed as rehearsal for dying (A Song for the Dead), and transcendence found only in narcotic static rather than anything solid (God Is in the Radio). Even its lullaby closer insists everything alive is just meat awaiting its turn (Mosquito Song) — beautiful sound as the delivery system for something poisonous."},"system-of-a-down~mezmerize":{"gist":"The first half of System of a Down's 2005 double statement — eleven tracks of warp-speed satire aimed at war and television. In rotation since 2010, nearly 400 plays deep.","by":"Opus","liner":"Mezmerize is a protest record that weaponizes its own swagger. Its rage runs on two rails — war and political hypocrisy in B.Y.O.B., Cigaro and the Soldier Side frame, where the poor are sent to die while presidents stay home; and media rot in Violent Pornography, Radio/Video and Lost in Hollywood, where television and celebrity are machines that sell cruelty and devour newcomers. The connective tissue is satire: macho bragging, disco choruses and party refrains deployed as Trojan horses so the mockery mirrors the numbing churn it indicts. Beneath the cartoon violence sits a real charge — a culture brainwashed, complacent, and blind to its own decline."},"type-o-negative~life-is-killing-me":{"gist":"Type O Negative's 2003 record — the last studio album, Peter Steele's gallows humor turned on his own failing body, doom-gothic and deadpan at once. Their biggest presence here by far: 850-plus plays running from 2011 to 2024.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Peter Steele's last studio album turns his hypochondria and mortality into gallows comedy: the title track rails at doctors as executioners and demands the right to die with dignity, while I Don't Wanna Be Me wishes the whole self away and asks for a party at the wake. Around that death-wish core sit its wounds and defenses — self-loathing in Less Than Zero and Anesthesia, revenge and stalker-cold detachment in ...A Dish Best Served Coldly and IYDKMIGTHTKY. What redeems it is the grief for the people who loved him, the shipyard father of Todd's Ship Gods and the saintly mother of Nettie.","arc":"The order hands the verdict to the closer — after all the failing-body comedy, The Dream Is Dead ends the record on lovelessness, as if what was killing him were never the body at all."},"chalk~crystalpunk":{"gist":"The Belfast trio's noise-pop statement — post-punk dread, dance-floor pulse and inherited scar tissue. A brand-new obsession: 180 plays, all of them in 2026.","by":"Opus","liner":"The Belfast noise-pop trio's record is haunted by inheritance and numbness — a father's leaving passed down like a curse in One-Nine-Eight-Zero, the city's literal bullet scars traced as birthright in Béal Feirste. Love here is a trap or a wound that has gone dead: a first secret same-sex feeling silenced into permanent numbness (Can't feel it), a lover whose words leave no dignified exit (Tongue), the helplessness of not being there when someone breaks (Pain). Even its defiance is armor, a disaffected shrug clinging to love as the one thing that insulates against the rest."},"soundgarden~a-sides":{"gist":"The 1997 singles compilation — Soundgarden's decade compressed into seventeen A-sides, Cornell's howl from Sub Pop scrap to arena weariness. A steady presence through the 2010s here, 375 plays deep.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A career-spanning singles set that, gathered together, reveals a band whose loudness was mostly a delivery system for depression. The heaviness stays constant while the mood underneath keeps returning to the same well: gloom arriving without cause on Fell on Black Days and Outshined, the wish to erase everything external on Blow Up the Outside World and Black Hole Sun, the caged animal bolting on Rusty Cage. Even the outward-facing songs — Cornell's environmental grief, his tribute to a spoon-playing busker — circle loving what's being lost. What holds the grab-bag together isn't a theme so much as a temperament: Gen-X despair given riffs, spite offered as release, communion found only in shared ruin.","arc":"Sequenced by release, the singles run doubles as the career in miniature — a decade of the same temperament refining, until the last word is bleeding together rather than raging alone."},"rammstein~reise-reise":{"gist":"Rammstein's 2004 album — the Berlin six-piece at their imperial phase, industrial marches wrapped around ballads and satire. Long-running company: 450 plays stretching from 2010 to 2025.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A record that keeps pushing love and appetite past the point where they become their own destruction. Again and again devotion turns lethal — the willing victim of Mein Teil, the partner walled up alive in Stein um Stein, the father who smothers his child on Dalai Lama, love as a devouring beast in Amour — desire and violence rendered with the same deadpan calm. Around that grim center sit the band's public poses: the tender ugliness of Morgenstern, the mourning of Ohne dich, and the satirical swagger of Amerika and Moskau, empire and lust sung with a wink. What unites it is a fascination with how far intimacy can be taken before it kills.","arc":"Near the end the deadpan drops — Ohne dich grieves with no irony at all — before Amour closes the case: love itself is the beast."},"machine-head~through-the-ashes-of-empires":{"gist":"Machine Head's 2003 comeback — the record that clawed back their credibility after the nu-metal detour, Robb Flynn turning private wreckage into weaponized resolve. A cornerstone here: nearly 1,100 plays across fifteen years.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A record about refusing to be broken, and about where the breaking started. The self-liberation anthems that anchor it — Imperium, Bite the Bullet, Wipe the Tears — all run the same conversion, turning fear, rage and years of rejection into fuel for taking one's life back. Underneath the defiance sits the wound that made it necessary: Left Unfinished and Days Turn Blue to Gray both reckon with an absent, addicted parent, vowing to become the opposite. Even the outward-facing songs, an ecological requiem and a promise of merciless vengeance for harmed innocents, carry that same protective fury.","arc":"The order steps off the battlefield at the last — Descend the Shades of Night sits down with Death and accepts the one ending the whole record fought to defy."},"northlane~alien":{"gist":"Northlane's 2019 album — the Sydney band rewired into industrial textures for Marcus Bridge's most personal material. Constant rotation since release: over 800 plays from 2019 to now.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Marcus Bridge's record, and everyone knows it: a scorched autobiography of a childhood in Sydney's Kings Cross under drug-addicted parents, and of surviving what it left behind. Bloodline, Freefall and Enemy of the Night lay out the raw scenes — inherited bad blood, a father's violent collapse, the numbness stamped in early — while Talking Heads and Jinn turn inward to the intrusive voices and walled-in dread that trauma seeded. The recurring vow is to carry the marks without becoming the person who made them, and Paradigm and Sleepless extend that ache outward to loving an addict and bracing, helplessly, for the fall.","arc":"For all the refusal, the running order circles home — the record ends inside Enemy of the Night, back at the father’s fall, the wound revisited rather than outrun."},"deftones~around-the-fur":{"gist":"Deftones' 1997 second album — the Sacramento band sharpening nu-metal's tools into something sensual and menacing. A slow-burn fixture here: nearly 400 plays spread across fourteen years.","by":"Opus","liner":"Chino Moreno's early masterwork of intimacy that can't tell affection from threat. Mascara, Dai the Flu and MX all braid lust with contempt and injury into one murmured line — a wife catalogued by her flaws, wounds reread as proof of love, a seduction tipping into the urge to hurt. Around it runs a matching need to disappear: the sunless private withdrawal of My Own Summer and the destination-less flight of Be Quiet and Drive, escape valued for its own sake. Grief surfaces too, in the screamed tribute of Headup. Throughout, feeling and menace breathe as one."},"a-2yw9ix~a-1ue2pqe":{"gist":"Midori's 2008 major-label debut — the Osaka jazz-punk band introducing themselves with a bow and a scream, Mariko Goto's piano-punk whiplash at full force. Found in 2021, deepest in 2026: just under 200 plays and climbing.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"The Osaka jazz-punk band's 2008 major-label debut is a record of narrators who cannot hold love and horror apart, and Mariko Goto sings each as if they were the same syllable. かなしい日々。 chants a murder-threat as its only vocabulary for devotion; ゆきこさん treats burning down a blemished life as the one honest way forward; お猿 lets a nursery-rhyme bounce carry a girl off to be trafficked overseas. Grief and self-loathing run through the rest — a dead father dared to dance over his grave in ちはるの恋, the too-smart cat begging for hell in ハウリング地獄. Feedback-hell self-portraits from someone sharp enough to name her own trap and unable to leave it.","arc":"The order runs from the record's only unguarded word — a one-line chant of love up front — down to the late howl begging for hell: devotion offered first, then dismantled."},"a-perfect-circle~emotive":{"gist":"A Perfect Circle's 2004 covers statement, released on US election day — peace anthems and protest songs recast into something colder. Steady company through the 2010s here: nearly 370 plays.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Released on US election day 2004, this is a covers record whose argument is the curation itself: Keenan gathers other people's peace anthems and protest songs and sings them into ambivalence, so that Imagine and What's Going On arrive as elegies for hopes already lost rather than pleas that expect an answer. The two originals sharpen the point, Passive turning the same charge of numb disengagement on a single person, Counting Bodies Like Sheep staging power as a lullaby that pacifies. Taken together the selections indict a nation that traded understanding for aggression, the recasting doing the accusing.","arc":"The order strips as it goes — the record ends a cappella on Mitchell's Fiddle and the Drum, the nation addressed directly once the music has been taken away."},"raubtier~det-finns-bara-krig":{"gist":"The Swedish industrial-metal duo's 2012 album — martial synth-thrash sung entirely in Swedish, the title declaring there is only war. A heavy-rotation fixture of the early library: over 500 plays between 2011 and 2018.","by":"Opus","liner":"The reads circle one conviction the title states outright: existence is war and nothing else, life reduced to struggle, hunger and an eternal deadly vigil. The martial machinery arrives as awe rather than horror, tanks sweating blood in Achtung Panzer, the mercenary of Legoknekt loving only cash and weapons and boasting he cannot truly die. Even when the frame narrows to one man, the predator's logic holds, whether the loyalty of Dobermann or the wolf-pack survival of Kamphund, and the closing apocalypses greet total ruin with relish, not dread."},"sodom~m-16":{"gist":"Sodom's 2001 Vietnam concept album — German thrash veterans walking a tour of duty from the draft to the body bags. Long-lived here: nearly 500 plays spread across twelve years.","by":"Opus","liner":"A Vietnam concept record that treats combat as the machinery for switching a human being off: across Among the Weirdcong and the title track the soldier reports his own atrocities with feelings frozen out, killing enemies he never sees and burying the shame rather than facing it. Sodom keep circling how faith and muddied thinking feed that machine, and where they widen the lens, Little Boy voicing the Hiroshima bomb over a sleeping child, the tenderness turns obscene. Cannon Fodder names the verdict plainly, heroism a lie and men spent for nothing."},"nine-inch-nails~pretty-hate-machine":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 1989 debut — Reznor's synth-driven first statement, industrial dread in pop shapes. A constant here: over 600 plays running from 2011 to the present.","by":"Opus","liner":"Reznor's debut opens with the one act of defiance it can muster and spends the rest of itself losing that fight. Head Like a Hole would rather die than kneel to money-as-god, but everywhere after, the surrender is inward and total: obsession that shames him, a lover permanently out of reach, purity stolen and mourned. Sanctified, Something I Can Never Have and Ringfinger all trace the same helpless slide, worship curdling into self-destruction, the will to resist worn down until submission itself becomes the appetite."},"marilyn-manson~the-pale-emperor":{"gist":"Marilyn Manson's 2015 blues turn — the shock apparatus swapped for swagger and fatigue. A concentrated obsession: ~350 plays packed into 2014-2016.","by":"Opus","liner":"Manson's blues turn keeps circling love as domination and mutual ruin, devotion measured in damage. Deep Six resists a mind-invading narcissist, Cupid Carries a Gun arms desire as something lethal, and Warship My Wreck offers injury as proof of feeling. Underneath the swagger the reads find a hollowed self that has been laid bare too often to open again, from the dissolving binge of Third Day of a Seven Day Binge to the mirror-and-mortality reckoning where even his defenses wear his own face. Persona as armor over exhaustion."},"pro8l3m~pro8l3m":{"gist":"The Warsaw duo's 2016 self-titled debut — Steez83's cinematic beats under Oskar's crime-noir testament. Nearly 340 plays since release; three album cuts never scrobbled here, so the picture is almost, not perfectly, complete.","by":"Opus","liner":"The Warsaw duo's debut is crime-rap noir where luxury is always built on dirty money and the swagger never quite covers the rot underneath. Molly and 777moneymaker chase the reckless high, but Dr Melfi and K-Pax keep exposing wealth as bottomless appetite, one more purchase from a happiness that never comes. The reads circle that hollow center relentlessly, and the record frames itself as a dying man's testament, from the bitter deathbed catalog of Intro to Outro's regret over knowingly choosing the wrong path."},"slayer~christ-illusion":{"gist":"Slayer's 2006 record — Lombardo back behind the kit, the provocation machinery aimed at faith and modern war at once. A heavy early-library staple: over 700 plays through the 2010s.","by":"Opus","liner":"The Lombardo-reunion record is Slayer swinging their two oldest sledgehammers, religion and war, harder than ever. Flesh Storm and Jihad treat modern combat and terror as media-fed spectacle voiced from inside the killing, while Cult and Skeleton Christ turn faith itself into the disease and pick damnation on purpose. When the crosshairs narrow to a single mind — the shell-shocked veteran of Eyes of the Insane, the ecstatic torturer of Catatonic — the provocation stops being a slogan and becomes a portrait of what violence does to a person."},"rammstein~rosenrot":{"gist":"Rammstein's 2005 album — offcuts from the Reise, Reise sessions grown into their own gallery of fatal desire. Long-running rotation: nearly 600 plays from 2010 to 2022.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Rammstein's sixth record works the seam where desire turns fatal or grotesque, sung largely in German. Rosenrot rebuilds a folk tale into a boy climbing to his death for a rose, Feuer und Wasser burns with a longing that can never mix, and the pitch-black Spring lets a crowd chant a man off a bridge for sport. Even the tenderness carries a blade — Stirb nicht vor mir threads a dream lover's hands around a throat — so that love, cruelty, and spectacle keep bleeding into one another.","arc":"The order ends on the wink — Ein Lied casts the band as pious servants of your ears, the spectacle taking a bow after all the fatal desire."},"carcass~heartwork":{"gist":"Carcass's 1993 turn from gore to melodic death metal — surgical vocabulary, ornate riffing. Around 400 plays here in the mid-2010s; the opening cut never scrobbled, so the picture is one track short.","by":"Opus","liner":"Heartwork is Carcass trading the pure gore of their earlier records for melodic death metal that aims its clinical, thesaurus-drunk language at human systems. The medical vocabulary of Carnal Forge renders mass death as industry, Arbeit Macht Fleisch twists a death-camp slogan into a meat grinder for labor, and Death Certificate flattens a whole life into a filed statistic. The throughline is dehumanization dressed in ornate words — love as a doomed transaction in No Love Lost, the title track staging beauty and corruption as one bleeding act."},"alice-in-chains~the-devil-put-dinosaurs-here":{"gist":"Alice in Chains' 2013 record — the second DuVall-era album, slow erosion in place of the old free-fall. Steady company: nearly 400 plays from 2013 to 2025.","by":"Opus","liner":"The second DuVall-era record circles a single sensation: being hollowed out and drained until color and meaning drain with it. Hollow sets the image of a drilled-through mountain, and the reads keep returning to a self too weathered to be claimed — Stone refusing to be carved into, Phantom Limb turning abandonment into a vow to haunt the betrayer. The one outward-facing swing, the title track's sneer at fundamentalist bigotry, only sharpens the contrast: the venom aimed at others is nothing next to the erosion turned inward."},"anthrax~the-greater-of-two-evils":{"gist":"Anthrax's 2004 re-recordings set — Belladonna-era classics re-cut with John Bush's voice. A well-worn early staple, ~370 plays through the 2010s; several cuts never scrobbled here, so the audit is partial.","by":"Opus","liner":"A re-recordings set — Belladonna-era classics re-cut with John Bush — so the meaning lives in what the band chose to revisit. Taken together the picks expose early Anthrax's two engines running side by side: pulp-adrenaline joyrides drawn from comics and speed (I Am the Law, Caught in a Mosh, Metal Thrashing Mad) and a stubborn moral conscience that refuses to look away (Indians, Keep It in the Family, Belly of the Beast). Revisited through Bush's harder voice, the compilation reads as the band auditing what it always was: goofball thrill and genuine outrage, never fully choosing between them."},"limp-bizkit~chocolate-starfish-and-the-hot-dog-flavored-water":{"gist":"Limp Bizkit's 2000 commercial summit — red cap, big riffs, bigger chip on the shoulder. Over 600 plays here from 2010 to 2024; one album cut never scrobbled.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Peak nu-metal Limp Bizkit, and the reads keep catching the same thing: swagger worn as armor. Most of the record is pure commercial-summit bravado, daring the crowd and anyone who'd test it (Rollin', My Generation, My Way), the boasting its own subject. But the cracks are what recur — Getcha Groove On's startling admission of being a wreck under the posturing, Hold On's one-sided devotion bleeding out — so that the thin-skinned Full Nelson grievance and the manifesto sneering all read as thick shell over something unsteady underneath.","arc":"For all the cracks, the order never lets the armor off — the record exits through a posse-cut remix and a stand-up routine, still in full costume at the door."},"linkin-park~hybrid-theory":{"gist":"Linkin Park's 2000 debut — the record that fused rap-metal, electronics and pop hooks into the defining sound of its moment. Second only to Meteora here: nearly 1,700 plays from 2010 to the present.","by":"Opus","liner":"The debut runs on a single accusation turned inward: the enemy is a second self, a face beneath the skin cataloguing every lie until daylight feels like betrayal. Papercut and By Myself trap the narrator in his own paranoid loop, unable to trust others or himself, while In the End tallies all that effort and comes up empty. Even when the blame points outward — a manipulator, a poisoned life — the wound is that he keeps wrecking himself to hold on, rage erupting because the words won't stop coming."},"nine-inch-nails~the-fragile":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 1999 double album — Reznor's sprawling, half-instrumental follow-up to The Downward Spiral. Deep-rooted here: over 1,300 plays across the library's whole life.","by":"Opus","liner":"A double album that breathes half in instrumentals, and the sung half keeps repeating one motion: a self coming apart faster than it can be held together. Somewhat Damaged and Into the Void map the collapse from the inside — abandoned, poisoned, gathering up fragments no longer allowed to stay — until the only exits left are drowning or surrender in The Great Below. The counterweight is devotion offered from inside the ruin: on the title track Reznor swears to shield someone luminous while admitting he is already past saving himself."},"machine-head~the-blackening":{"gist":"Machine Head's 2007 epic — eight songs stretched to thrash-opera length, the band's critical peak. Over 1,100 plays from 2010 on.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"An epic-length thrash record whose fury is almost entirely aimed outward — at war profiteering and patriotic manipulation on Clenching the Fists of Dissent and A Farewell to Arms, at the religious right on Halo, at a critic who slandered a murdered friend on Aesthetics of Hate. Even the inward-turned songs read as damage inflicted from outside: the mirror-shattering despair of Beautiful Mourning, the outcast solidarity of Wolves, a brotherhood that finds ferocity in shared rejection but never satisfaction. Rage as principled protest, marshalled at length rather than vented.","arc":"The order files the fury out into mourning — the record ends inside A Farewell to Arms, ten minutes of grief where the rage finally hands over."},"nine-inch-nails~broken":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 1992 EP — the furious, guitar-first middle finger recorded in secret mid-label-war. Nearly 1,100 plays here.","by":"Opus","liner":"The rage EP compresses to a single downward motion: self-disgust that wants to be used up and thrown away. Wish and Gave Up hit rock bottom and smash everything they believed in, faith in God and partner both gone, arriving at resigned surrender after trying and failing. Happiness in Slavery pushes it outward — conformity as a deadening comfort, a person ground down into a spare part of a broken machine — but the machine here is mostly the self, addiction and blasphemy folding pleasure into filth with no rescue coming."},"nine-inch-nails~year-zero":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2007 dystopian concept album — sixteen transmissions from a burning near-future America, released with an alternate-reality game around it. Over 900 plays here.","by":"Opus","liner":"The dystopian concept record ventriloquizes the collapse: Reznor keeps stepping inside the voices that cause the ruin — the greed dressed as accountability in Capital G, the demagogue's righteous us-against-them creed in God Given — and the sting is always that this voice lives in the listener too. Around them a burning America runs its countdown, survivalism and outsourced killing, until The Warning hands humanity an outside ultimatum: a parasite species, reform or be erased. What lingers past the judgment is the quiet comfort offered at the end, tenderness sung straight into the apocalypse."},"limp-bizkit~gold-cobra":{"gist":"Limp Bizkit's 2011 reunion record — the classic lineup back for one more lap of maximum bravado. Nearly 900 plays from release to the present.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"A reunion record that is mostly one register turned to maximum — braggadocious battle-rap dominance, one king on the hill threatening rivals and taunting jealous haters across Gold Cobra, Shark Attack, Why Try, and the party-hype of 90.2.10. The interest is in the cracks the swagger can't quite paper over: Loser and Walking Away concede a relationship that surfaces everyone's worst, and My Own Cobain drops the bluster entirely for a genuinely wounded reach after a private breaking point. Hedonism as armour, with real hurt leaking through the seams.","arc":"The back half is where the shell thins — Loser, Walking Away and My Own Cobain stack the record’s late stretch, the bravado spending itself before the exit."},"system-of-a-down~system-of-a-down":{"gist":"System of a Down's 1998 debut — the rawest, strangest version of the band, surrealism and fury not yet radio-shaped. Nearly 800 plays here.","by":"Opus","liner":"A debut that weaponizes surreal free-association into political attack, careening between blasphemous provocation (Suite-Pee), free-verse riddling (Know, Darts), and pointed rage. Beneath the deliberate nonsense sits a coherent target: systems that manipulate and consume — holy war on War?, surveillance on Spiders, childhood abuse on Mind — building toward P.L.U.C.K.'s blunt demand for recognition of the Armenian Genocide. Elsewhere the chaos turns personal, Sugar's manic sprint through a fraying mind and Soil's raw grief for a friend lost to suicide. Absurdist crudeness as a delivery system for genuine fury."},"nine-inch-nails~the-slip":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2008 record — written and released free in a matter of weeks, lean and unpolished by design. Over 750 plays here.","by":"Opus","liner":"A late, lean Reznor record that splits its attention between political indictment and interior collapse. Letting You and Head Down turn outward at fear-mongering power and the complicity that sustains it, while the sung core keeps circling loss of self-control — Discipline's craving for someone else to impose limits, Echoplex's retreat into an unreachable inner fortress, 1,000,000's total numbed detachment. Demon Seed names the recurring danger directly: a compulsion he can no longer contain finally embraced. Systems that grind people down, and a self that quietly stops resisting."},"northlane~obsidian":{"gist":"Northlane's 2022 record — the full swing into industrial electronics after Alien's autobiography. In heavy rotation since release: 900 plays.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"The electronic-forward record where the interrogations turn inward as often as out. Half of it is reckoning with people who rewrote the speaker's history and cast him as the villain — Clarity's demand not to be gaslit, Carbonized's promise to expose a predator's smoke and mirrors — and the other half is the wreckage that leaves behind: the self-disgust of Abomination, the drained circling of Is This a Test?. Xen offers the closest thing to a way out, insisting meaning is a private connection nobody else has to ratify.","arc":"For all Xen’s offered exit, the order ends still circling — Dark Solitaire wanders out half-hoping to be found, the way out named but not yet taken."},"wargasm-uk~venom":{"gist":"WARGASM's 2023 debut mixtape-album — the UK duo's electronicore rush of poison-as-power. A fast obsession: over 740 plays.","by":"Opus","liner":"A debut that runs on weaponized damage — the duo take everything that would make them feared or othered and flip it into swagger and predatory pride, poison reframed as power on the title track. The sex is inseparable from violence and speed (Ride The Thunder, Death Rattle's romanticized apocalypse), and even the love songs treat devotion as running toward a mutual wreck, Sonic Dog Tag grinding two incompatible people down until crashing and letting go become the same release. Underneath the bravado is real self-loathing it keeps daring you to mistake for confidence."},"serj-tankian~elect-the-dead":{"gist":"Serj Tankian's 2007 solo debut — the SOAD voice unaccompanied, protest songs and broken love songs sharing one throat. Over 730 plays across the years.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Tankian's first solo record swings between furious protest and fractured love songs, often in the same breath. The political side indicts a docile populace and manufactured wars — The Unthinking Majority's medicated leash, Feed Us casting leaders as parasites, Honking Antelope mourning what human greed erases. But the personal collapses run just as bleak, from Lie Lie Lie's smiling murder to Empty Walls, where an anti-war lament turns out to hinge on someone who killed the singer's family — the abstraction grounded in the genocide his people carry.","arc":"The order strips both threads at the last — the title track lets lover and love dissolve until wanting nothing is what remains, protest and heartbreak closing on the same empty room."},"nine-inch-nails~ghosts-i-iv":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2008 instrumental experiment — 36 numbered pieces, released Creative Commons, the first volume free. Over 1,000 plays here: the library's biggest wordless record.","by":"Opus","liner":"Not songs but a 36-track instrumental experiment — Reznor and collaborators improvising in the studio and shaping the fragments into ambient, textural pieces, numbered rather than named so nothing tells you what to feel. It moves through hushed drones, corroded electronics and slow-building menace across its four volumes, closer to a sketchbook of moods than an album with an argument. It was also a release-model statement: put out independently under a Creative Commons license, the first volume free, the rest for sale — sound left deliberately open, meaning left to the listener."},"joel-nielsen~black-mesa-soundtrack":{"gist":"Joel Nielsen's score for Black Mesa, the fan-built remake of Half-Life — tension, ambience and military-electronic drive cued to the facility's collapse. Over 850 plays: the sound of many hours in that world.","by":"Opus","liner":"A score, not an album — Nielsen's music for the fan remake of Half-Life, built to carry you through the Black Mesa facility rather than to say anything on its own. Its job is atmosphere and pacing: ambient dread in the research halls, ratcheting tension and military-electronic drive as the place comes apart, cues named for the levels they underscore (Surface Tension, Questionable Ethics, We've Got Hostiles). The one moment with a voice, Forget About Freeman, sets the game's radio order to abandon the base to music — the sound of being written off, someone else's story doing the talking."},"nine-inch-nails~beside-you-in-time":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2007 concert film — the With Teeth arena tour captured whole, all fog and strobe. What lives here are its video and rehearsal versions, nearly 950 plays of them: a performance document whose songs keep their reads on the studio records."},"tool~opiate":{"gist":"Tool's 1992 debut EP — six early cuts, two of them live, the rage still simple and outward. A hundred-odd plays here, the roots of the catalogue.","by":"Opus","liner":"The debut EP is Tool with the rage still pointed outward, mostly at authority and the failures of justice. The title track voices a predatory preacher weaponizing faith to keep the flock docile; Hush turns censorship into pure provocation, the forbidden speech becoming the whole song; Jerk-Off, live and seething, watches wrong go unpunished until patience curdles into a homicidal impulse. Even the interior cuts, Sweat's dissolving consciousness and Part of Me's silent inner fragment, share the same appetite for domination and confrontation, a band already lunging at whatever demands blind obedience."},"tool~undertow":{"gist":"Tool's 1993 full-length debut — the grinding, unadorned first statement. Around 120 plays across the years.","by":"Opus","liner":"Tool's full-length debut is a descent that keeps refusing the narrator a clean conscience. Whether he's admitting complicity in the liar he tolerated on Intolerance, confessing himself the hopeless deceiver of Sober, or converting an abuser's venom into fuel on Bottom, survival here means self-implication rather than judgment. The recurring dread is trauma's logic — harm learned early reenacted as intimacy and control — and Disgustipated's mock-sermon drags that violence all the way down to the ecology of life feeding on life."},"tool~nima":{"gist":"Tool's 1996 breakthrough — the record where the interludes, pranks and shadow-work fused into one architecture. The most-played Tool record here: over 400 plays.","by":"Opus","liner":"Ænima's obsession is molting — burrowing through the buried, shameful self to force a painful transformation, most nakedly on Forty Six & 2 with its Jungian shadow-work and dream of chromosomal evolution, and again on jimmy's reach back to reunite with a boyhood self stalled at eleven. Around that core the reads circle numbness and cleansing: Stinkfist digging ever deeper just to feel, Ænema praying for a flood to wash a vapid Los Angeles away, Third Eye recovering a forgotten identity through altered perception. The spoken pranks and found-audio interludes stitch it into a single deliberate architecture."},"tool~lateralus":{"gist":"Tool's 2001 record — prog-metal as spiritual instruction, spirals and surrender. Nearly 300 plays here.","by":"Opus","liner":"Tool's spiritual-geometry record keeps circling one instruction: stop thinking your way through, and surrender to the body and the present. The title track builds that argument on the Fibonacci spiral, Parabola recasts pain as illusion and breath as sacred, and Reflection stages the ego-death the whole album is pushing toward, its narrator realizing his light was only borrowed. Even the grievances — the self-imprisoning resentment of The Grudge, the drained fury of Ticks & Leeches — resolve toward release, letting the weather do what weather does while the instrumentals breathe between."},"tool~10-000-days":{"gist":"Tool's 2006 record — the grief album, named for the years Judith Marie kept her faith. Over 440 plays, the second-deepest Tool well here.","by":"Opus","liner":"Two records braided into one: a private grief and a species-wide verdict. At its center sit Wings for Marie, Pt. 1 and 10,000 Days (Wings, Pt. 2), Keenan's bedside elegy for his mother Judith, who kept unbroken faith through decades of paralysis while her son doubted he deserved her prayers — a life dared to demand its wings. Around that core the album turns outward and cold, indicting the voyeur who feeds on televised death in Vicarious and the finger-pointing hypocrite in The Pot, until Right in Two watches from the angels' vantage as a species handed reason chooses only to divide and kill. Devotion and contempt, sharing the same breath."},"tool~fear-inoculum":{"gist":"Tool's 2019 return after thirteen years — long-form patience turned on slow poisons. A modest two dozen plays here: respected more than lived-in.","by":"Opus","liner":"The thirteen-year-wait record turns its prog-metal patience toward poisons that work slowly — manipulators whose calm precedes the wreckage, minds that curdle into their own worst jury. The title track frames it as immunology: exhaling a contagion once naively welcomed, building resistance to a deceiver who feeds on fear. Culling Voices tracks that same excavation inward, a paranoid court convicting on whispers alone, while 7empest rejects the staged serenity of someone whose storm was never in doubt. Set against these, Pneuma reaches the other way — spirit pinned to flesh but straining past it, all souls born from one breath, a genuine mystical longing for awakening."},"serj-tankian~harakiri":{"gist":"Serj Tankian's 2012 record — his fastest and angriest solo work, written in the season of mass animal die-offs. Nearly 400 plays.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"Fast, furious, and read through the era of mass animal die-offs that gives the title track its dread — beached fish and blackbirds dropping from the sky taken as omens of a civilization that raised monuments to itself and ignored the wise. Around Harakiri's ultimatum to love one another or perish together, the rage spreads outward: Cornucopia mourns a broken pact with an Earth reaped in storms, Figure It Out names executives as the actual disease, and Occupied Tears turns grief over the Israeli-Palestinian conflict into a plea for a shared homeland. Ecological warning and political fury run on the same current, always circling back to a species wounding itself with deceit.","arc":"The order ends at the loom — Weave On leaves the sisters weaving the species' bloody record, the day's omens filed into the long tapestry."},"serj-tankian~imperfect-harmonies":{"gist":"Serj Tankian's 2010 record — orchestras and electronics wrapped around the solo voice at its most operatic. Around 135 plays.","by":"Opus","liner":"Tankian's orchestral-electronic solo turn runs two threads at once: apocalyptic politics and intimate, unworthy love, delivered in operatic swings. Borders Are... names fear as the engine of separation and Left of Center skewers empire and theocracy, while Yes, It's Genocide strips an Armenian lament to pure liturgical repetition. Against that public fury runs private rescue — the pledges and self-doubt of Deserving?, the obsessive fixation that turns a whole city into one face on Beatus. The apocalyptic and the romantic keep bleeding into each other, catastrophe and devotion arriving in the same theatrical breath."},"a-2yw9ix~shinsekai":{"gist":"Midori's 2010 farewell — released months before the split, the Osaka persona mapped onto real streets one last time. Nearly 190 plays, most of them recent.","by":"Opus","liner":"The band's farewell record maps Mariko Goto's manic Osaka persona onto real geography and a nervous system that won't obey. Love here is a diagnosis the obsessive delivers on herself: the numb self-verdict of mediocrity on 凡庸VS茫洋, the second-floor dive at a lover on 鉄塔の上の2人, want so total it turns her into an object on あたし、ギターになっちゃった!!!!!. Where the impulse is the whole point the songs simply refuse to develop — メカ chants a robot's name into a wall for sixty characters, and どんぞこ hammers rock bottom into a nonsense rhyme so she doesn't sink through it."},"a-2yw9ix~a-3o8es":{"gist":"Midori's 2005 first mini-album — the Shimizu EP, the rawest recordings of the method already fully formed. A small, late-found treasure here.","by":"Opus","liner":"The earliest, rawest Midori material already has Goto's whole method in place: adoration and the wish to smash everything are the same feeling. Both takes of 愛って悲しいね shriek total devotion over last-train gossip about cheating and dread, landing on how sad it is to love at all, while グッバイ voices the exhaustion of a self-described liar whose I-love-yous have worn down into reflex. Elsewhere the appetite runs unapologetic — the plain summer lust of ロマンティック夏モード, the run at oblivion on 犬、走る。 where nihilism is worn as freedom."},"a-2yw9ix~swing":{"gist":"Midori's 2006 second mini — three songs between collapse and propulsion. A miniature, kept for its concentration.","by":"Opus","liner":"A small, three-track record that catches Goto between collapse and propulsion. swing narrows to one aching syllable before curdling into a predator's coo over a cornered rabbit, あかん!! flips exhaustion into launch in an explosively brief cry, and 朽ちては果てぬ drifts outward as a consciousness that rots but won't cleanly dissolve. Want, rupture, and endless decay, sketched in miniature."},"tool":{"gist":"The Los Angeles four-piece who turned metal into slow ritual — five albums across three decades, each built to be entered rather than consumed. Over 1,500 plays here, spread across the library's whole life.","by":"Opus · Fable","portrait":"Tool's body of work is one long excavation — the same drive to dig through the buried, shameful self, only the direction of the digging changes. On the early records the rage points outward at authority and inherited trauma, from Opiate's weaponized faith to Undertow's refusal of a clean conscience; then Ænima turns the shovel inward, making molting and shadow-work the whole subject. Lateralus answers its own excavation by telling the mind to stop and surrender to the body, and 10,000 Days braids that inward turn into private grief and species-wide contempt sharing one breath. Across a discography paced at five records in thirty years, the slowness is the argument: transformation as something you survive rather than achieve.","note":"Worth holding loosely — this is a band that boobytraps interpretation for sport, from hidden tracks to hoax lyrics to Keenan’s flat refusals to explain. The excavation reading is well-grounded in the songs; just know the diggers salted the site."},"serj-tankian":{"gist":"System of a Down's voice gone solo — protest opera in rock, orchestral and electronic clothing, the politics never off duty. Nearly 1,300 plays across three records here.","by":"Opus · Fable","portrait":"Freed from System of a Down, Tankian keeps writing as if catastrophe and devotion were the same event — apocalyptic politics and intimate, unworthy love arriving in the same theatrical breath. Elect The Dead already fuses the two, protest and heartbreak dissolving into one empty room, and Imperfect Harmonies pushes that fusion into operatic swings where a whole city collapses into one beloved face. The obsession underneath is a species wounding itself through deceit, whether the deceit is manufactured war or a smiling lie between lovers. By Harakiri the ecological turns literal, mass die-offs read as omens, the ultimatum plain: love one another or perish together, the personal and the planetary finally the same verdict.","note":"The engine under all of it is Armenia — grandson of genocide survivors, decades of recognition activism. The deceit the records keep indicting is, first and always, the denied crime his family carried; the politics and the love songs both inherit that wound."},"nine-inch-nails~hesitation-marks":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2013 record — the sober return, dread rendered in clean lines and drum machines. Over 670 plays here.","by":"Opus · Fable","liner":"The sober-middle-age NIN record keeps circling one question: who is left after the self comes apart. The speaker is a degraded reproduction on Copy of A, split into irreconcilable halves on In Two, and can no longer trust his own perception across Various Methods of Escape. What saves it from mere collapse is the surrender that arrives by While I'm Still Here, where dread finally softens into a plea to be kept company as everything winds down.","arc":"The plea isn’t the last word — Black Noise closes the record wordless and bleak, the comfort dissolving back into static."},"nine-inch-nails~add-violence":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2017 EP — the middle chapter of the Trilogy, simulation-dread in five tracks. Around 140 plays.","by":"Opus","liner":"The middle chapter of the Trilogy trades industrial fury for simulation-dread: a slow dissolving of the self and the world it thought was solid. Less Than mocks the empty promise that getting what you want will fix what's broken, The Lovers narrates the terror of no longer knowing who's driving, and This Isn't the Place sits inside the wrong-place shock of sudden grief. Everything folds toward the closer's decaying loop, refusing exit and asking, over and over, whether this is really what you wanted."},"nine-inch-nails~bad-witch":{"gist":"Nine Inch Nails' 2018 Trilogy closer — half an hour, saxophones, Bowie's shadow. A hundred-odd plays.","by":"Opus","liner":"The short, sax-scarred close of the Trilogy is Reznor watching a self curdle into something monstrous and alien, half-horrified and half-thrilled. Shit Mirror welcomes an unrecognizable transformation while confessing to a body left behind, Ahead of Ourselves jeers at a species too stupid to save itself, and God Break Down the Door — Bowie's late shadow clearly on it — arrives at a dissociated peace, no longer of this world. It ends in Over and Out's unresolving loop, forward motion dissolved into a curse of always being a decade ahead."},"massive-attack~100th-window":{"gist":"Massive Attack's 2003 album — effectively a 3D solo record, the paranoia whispered rather than sung. Nearly 190 plays.","by":"Opus","liner":"Effectively a 3D solo record, it distills trip-hop's paranoia to a numbed whisper — surveillance-era dread where intimacy, addiction and self-erasure keep blurring into one another. Future Proof drifts through a hollowed-out, chemically anaesthetised life, Butterfly Caught turns adoration predatory, and the closing Antistar begs to be reset to nothing, love offered as narcosis. Sinéad O'Connor's three vocals lend the album its conscience, most piercingly on A Prayer for England's grief-stricken plea against the killing of children."},"massive-attack~blue-lines":{"gist":"Massive Attack's 1991 debut — Bristol sound-system culture pressed into an album, the blueprint for everything called trip-hop. A modest fifty-odd plays here: visited, not lived in.","by":"Opus","liner":"The debut where a Bristol sound-system crew becomes an album, longing routed through a rotating cast rather than a single voice — Shara Nelson undone by love in Unfinished Sympathy, Horace Andy's reggae falsetto lifting the battered hope of Hymn of the Big Wheel, 3D and Tricky trading floating reverie and street-level menace. The record keeps circling desire and self-possession under threat: daydreaming as survival, a ferocious vow to protect what's yours in Safe From Harm, even a soul cover held up as quiet defiance. The passed mic is the form, the yearning the constant."},"massive-attack~protection":{"gist":"Massive Attack's 1994 album — the soft one, Tracey Thorn and Nicolette joining the rotation. Fifty-odd plays here.","by":"Opus","liner":"Massive Attack's softest record makes tenderness its argument — shelter offered without asking who conventionally does the shielding, Tracey Thorn's title vocal rendering mutual care as something deeper than romance. Around that warmth the reads keep finding people who can't be reached: the guarded, depleting stupor of Karmacoma, a partner's plea for freedom answered without bitterness in Better Things, Horace Andy hunted by watchful eyes in Spying Glass, Tricky and Nicolette mapping a paranoid European drift. The new voices in the rotation don't guest so much as widen the record's account of who needs protecting."},"massive-attack~heligoland":{"gist":"Massive Attack's 2010 album — the late return to songs, guest voices holding collapse at arm's length. Nearly 200 plays.","by":"Opus","liner":"The late return to song-form, outside vocalists pulled into a rotation that holds collapse close: Tunde Adebimpe's drought-prayer, Guy Garvey's strangled falsetto voicing a war-scarred man riveting everything into a bulletproof love in Flat of the Blade, Damon Albarn suspended in doubt. The reads keep circling entropy observed from both a warm bed and a cosmic distance — the personal running alongside the systemic in Splitting the Atom, desire as consuming sin in Paradise Circus, post-9/11 rendition dread in Atlas Air. Love recurs less as rescue than as the one shield raised against dissolution."},"nine-inch-nails":{"gist":"Trent Reznor's project — three decades of self-demolition set to machines, synth-pop to film-score austerity. One of the deepest wells in this library: nearly 12,000 plays across everything he has released.","by":"Opus · Fable","portrait":"Reznor has spent three decades documenting a single event in slow motion: a self hollowing itself out, and the compulsion that keeps him staring into the wreckage to see if anyone is still home. Pretty Hate Machine already surrenders inward after one act of defiance, The Downward Spiral turns that surrender into a clinical descent toward annihilation, and the late records only relocate the ruin — Hesitation Marks asking who is left after the collapse, the Trilogy watching the self curdle into something alien. Even the outward-facing work bends back to it: Year Zero's dystopia lodges the corrupting voice inside the listener. The wordless Ghosts and the concert document don't break the thesis so much as expose its floor — the dread survives even when the words go silent, the songs merely staged.","note":"The other half of the story is the delivery system — the label war that birthed Broken in secret, Year Zero’s alternate-reality game, Ghosts and The Slip given away under Creative Commons, and the film-score life that taught late NIN its textures. The records kept interrogating how music reaches you even as the words interrogated the self."}};
